2 CONT
2 CONT После большевистского переворота 1918г. сначала выехал с родителями в Францию, в 20-ых годах переехали в США. Президент Всемирного Клуба Нардов (World Backgammon Club), его называли "отцом современных нардов".
2 CONT
2 CONT Prince Alexis Obolensky Jr. (April 20, 1915 – February 8, 1986), a Russian-American socialite and sometimes called the "father of modern backgammon," was a member of the princely Obolensky family of the Rurik Dynasty.
2 CONT
2 CONT Prince Alexis Obolensky, a member of a Russian émigré family and president of the World Backgammon Club, an organization in Manhattan that sponsors international tournaments, died Saturday at his home in Manhattan. He was 71 years
2 CONC  old and also lived in Palm Beach, Fla.
2 CONT
2 CONT Prince Obolensky came here from France with his family in the 20's. He graduated from the University of Virginia.
2 CONT
2 CONT Surviving are two daughters, Anne, of Palm Beach, and Mary Underwood of London; a son, Alexis Jr., of Aspen, Colo.; three sisters, Alexandra Sazonoff and Mrs. David B. Morgan Jr., both of Asheville, N.C., and Princess Luba Trubetk
2 CONC soy of Sea Cliff, L.I., and a grandchild.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Prince_Alexis_Obolensky
